0..77 Comment "// The circle as the book states it: one point, one loop at it, and the loops"
78..157 Comment "// at the point equivalent to the integers, `Path(Circle, Base, Base) ≃ Int`."
158..231 Comment "// The loop is a free invertible cell at the point, so the paths from the"
232..308 Comment "// point to itself are the words over the loop and its inverse, with the two"
309..384 Comment "// cancellations as rules: the lowering mints them, and `Path(Circle, Base,"
385..462 Comment "// Base)` is that presented type. The integers are presented the same way, so"
463..541 Comment "// the winding number folds the words into them, spinning folds them back, and"
542..619 Comment "// both round trips are proved by induction over each presented type, with no"
620..698 Comment "// interval and nothing owed. `loop-space.gandr` states the same mechanism one"
699..725 Comment "// stratum down, on terms."
726..728 Comment "//"
729..794 Comment "// The operations on paths are the book\'s own, each under `Path`:"
795..871 Comment "// `Path.step(p, q)`, `Path.back(p)` and `Path.ap(f, p)` stand in a body and"
872..947 Comment "// in a type, each reading its path\'s type off the words it acts on — the"
948..1025 Comment "// loop, a word, or another operation over one — and `Path.ap` its codomain"
1026..1071 Comment "// off the function\'s written result. Nothing"
1072..1143 Comment "// here defines them or annotates them. The alternative, each operation"
1144..1219 Comment "// written where a path type is stated, repeats what the words already say."
1220..1294 Comment "// Reversal: a cell between two distinct points, whose words spell neither"
1295..1359 Comment "// endpoint, would need its operations\' path type written again."
1361..1436 Comment "// The circle, the presented integers, the winding number and spinning with"
1437..1458 Comment "// their round trips."
